system level commands

1)uname command tells which platform this is.
2)uptime tell us how long this instace is running
3)date show date current
4)who tells which users logged in at what time, how may user are there
5)whoami current user who is loggedin show only one user details
6)which show where is the location of that command
    which bash
7)id to see the user and id or users, groupid as well
8)sudo : superuser with all permission
9)shutdown need sudo to shutdown
10)reboot to restart with sudo

applation packege manager:
1)apt command is application package manager( used to install tools or apps) need root access 
    sudo apt install docker.io (installs docker if already present in the machine)
    else
    sudo apt-get install docker.io (-get will use internet to get docker from internet)
    sudo apt-get update (update the package system)
    sudo apt remove docker.io (remove the docker)

2)yum:lentos dnf pacman:arch portage:gento all these are package manager for different linux types

USER and Group management commands:

sudo useradd -m UserName add user to the linux.
    this create user

sudo passwd UserName
    this add password to user

su UserName
    this will help to switch user (somecases password will be required)
    this switch to secondary users

exit command will get you back to primary user

sudo userdel UserName delete the user

sudo groupadd GroupName
    check it cat/etc/group
    this creats group

sudo gpasswd -a UserName GroupName  
    this add the user to group

sudo gpasswd -M Multiple_UserNames GroupName
    this adds multiple user to group at once

sudo groupdel GroupName


File Permission commands:

1)chmod 777 file_name(full access to user , group, other user)

2)umask 0002 : for default file what should be file permissions by default

3)sudo chown user-name file-name. extension
    this changes the file ownership

4)sudo chgrp group-name file-name.extension


